Feature: Automate migration of eligible files from Purgo S3 landing folder to archive folder using Databricks PySpark

  Background:
    Given the Databricks secret scope "aws_keys" contains "access_key" and "secret_key"
    And the Unity Catalog is "purgo_databricks" and Schema is "purgo_playground"
    And the table "s3_file_process_log" exists in the schema "purgo_playground"
    And the table "s3_file_process_log" has columns: file_id (string), file_name (string), file_status (string), s3_landing_path (string), s3_archive_path (string), processed_timestamp (timestamp)
    And the S3 path format is "s3://<bucket>/<folder>/<subfolder>"
    And only files with file_status = "SUCCESS" are eligible for migration

  Scenario: Migrate eligible files from landing to archive folder (happy path)
    Given there are files in "s3_file_process_log" with file_status = "SUCCESS"
    And each file has a valid s3_landing_path and s3_archive_path
    When the PySpark script is executed
    Then the script reads AWS credentials from Databricks secret scope "aws_keys"
    And the script queries "purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" for files with file_status = "SUCCESS"
    And for each eligible file, the script moves the file from s3_landing_path to s3_archive_path
    And the file is deleted from the source s3_landing_path after successful copy
    And the file appears in the target s3_archive_path with the same file_name
    And the script logs the migration event with file_id, file_name, source, target, and timestamp

  Scenario Outline: Data-driven migration of multiple eligible files
    Given the following files exist in "s3_file_process_log":
      | file_id | file_name         | file_status | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | <file_id> | <file_name>     | <file_status> | <s3_landing_path>                                         | <s3_archive_path>                                            |
    When the PySpark script is executed
    Then only files with file_status = "SUCCESS" are migrated from <s3_landing_path> to <s3_archive_path>
    And the file <file_name> is deleted from <s3_landing_path> after migration
    And the file <file_name> exists in <s3_archive_path>
    And the migration event is logged with file_id, file_name, source, target, and timestamp

    Examples:
      | file_id | file_name         | file_status | s3_landing_path                                              | s3_archive_path                                               |
      | 001     | patient1.csv      | SUCCESS     | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive |
      | 002     | patient2.csv      | FAILED      | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive |
      | 003     | patient3.csv      | SUCCESS     | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive |

  Scenario: Error when AWS credentials are missing in Databricks secret scope
    Given the Databricks secret scope "aws_keys" does not contain "access_key" or "secret_key"
    When the PySpark script is executed
    Then the script fails with error message "AWS credentials not found in Databricks secret scope: aws_keys"

  Scenario: Error when s3_landing_path or s3_archive_path is invalid
    Given a file in "s3_file_process_log" with file_status = "SUCCESS"
    And the s3_landing_path or s3_archive_path does not match the format "s3://<bucket>/<folder>/<subfolder>"
    When the PySpark script attempts migration
    Then the script skips the file and logs error "Invalid S3 path format for file_id: <file_id>"

  Scenario: Error when file does not exist in s3_landing_path
    Given a file in "s3_file_process_log" with file_status = "SUCCESS"
    And the file <file_name> does not exist in <s3_landing_path>
    When the PySpark script attempts migration
    Then the script logs error "File not found in source S3 path for file_id: <file_id>, file_name: <file_name>"

  Scenario: Error when file already exists in s3_archive_path
    Given a file in "s3_file_process_log" with file_status = "SUCCESS"
    And the file <file_name> already exists in <s3_archive_path>
    When the PySpark script attempts migration
    Then the script overwrites the file in <s3_archive_path>
    And logs warning "File overwritten in archive S3 path for file_id: <file_id>, file_name: <file_name>"

  Scenario: Error when file_status is not "SUCCESS"
    Given a file in "s3_file_process_log" with file_status != "SUCCESS"
    When the PySpark script is executed
    Then the script does not migrate the file
    And logs info "File skipped due to file_status: <file_status> for file_id: <file_id>"

  Scenario: Error when S3 permissions are insufficient
    Given a file in "s3_file_process_log" with file_status = "SUCCESS"
    And the AWS credentials do not have permission to read from <s3_landing_path> or write to <s3_archive_path>
    When the PySpark script attempts migration
    Then the script fails with error message "Insufficient S3 permissions for file_id: <file_id>"

  Scenario: Logging migration results
    Given the PySpark script migrates files from s3_landing_path to s3_archive_path
    When migration is successful
    Then the script updates the migration log with file_id, file_name, source, target, status "ARCHIVED", and processed_timestamp
    And if migration fails, the script logs the error with file_id, file_name, error_message, and processed_timestamp

  Scenario Outline: Validation of S3 path formats
    Given the following S3 paths are provided in "s3_file_process_log":
      | s3_path                                                      |
      | <s3_path>                                                    |
    When the PySpark script validates the S3 path format
    Then the path must match the regex "^s3://[a-zA-Z0-9\-]+(/[a-zA-Z0-9_\-]+)+$"
    And if the path does not match, the script logs error "Invalid S3 path format: <s3_path>"

    Examples:
      | s3_path                                                      |
      | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw    |
      | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive|
      | s3:/agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw     |
      | agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw         |
